    "lawCode": "Add §42-a, Lien L",
    "lawSection": "Lien Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S663\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the lien law, in relation to authorizing a lien for\ncommon charges owed to a homeowners' association\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nAuthorizes a lien for unpaid common charges owed to a homeowners'\nassociation.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nSection 1: Adds a new section 42-a to the Lien Law. This provision\nadds homeowners' associations to the list of entities that can file a\nlien to collect homeowners' association common charges owed to such\nassociation.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\nCurrently, homeowners' associations that have levied common charges\nmust go to either small claims court or the county court to collect\ncommon charges. This practice simply increases the costs of collection\nwhich must be borne by all other members of the homeowners'\nassociation.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nHomeowners' associations have the responsibility of maintaining the\nneighborhood in which its members live. Some of these responsibilities\ninclude snow plowing, road repair, sanitation, signage, lighting, and\ncommon grounds maintenance. In order to provide these services,\nhomeowners associations must charge its members a common charge.\n\nThe reason for this bill is to provide such associations with a more\nreasonable mechanism for placing liens on a members' property that has\nnot paid their common charges. Currently, much time and expense is\nspent in court by having to initiate and proceed with a civil action\nagainst the property owner. The average time its takes a homeowners'\nassociation in litigation against one homeowner can be as long as 2 to\n3 years. During this time period, an attorney needs to appear in court\nmultiple times to obtain a judgment. Once a judgment is obtained, then\nthe association must collect on this judgment. This bill would give\nhomeowners associations the same right as condominium owners\nassociations to file a lien in the office in which the deed to the\nreal property is filed instead of going through the judicial process.\nThe homeowners association lien on real property for unpaid common\ncharges will be done in the same manner as a mechanic's lien.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nS.7299/A.10465 of 2006, S.645-A of 2007/2008\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n LOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\n60 days after it shall have become law.\n",
